WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.251
Munich: The Edge of War

00:00:01.335 --> 00:00:02.669
is the story
of two young men

00:00:02.753 --> 00:00:04.796
embroiled in one of the most
crucial moments

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.715
in 20th century history.

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:08.425
Adapted
from Robert Harris' novel,

00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:11.386
the film is set
in the autumn of 1938,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.472
as the British Prime
Minister desperately tries

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:14.890
to broker a peace.

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:17.768
As long as war
has not begun,

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:22.189
there is always hope
that it may be prevented.

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:24.358
Meanwhile,
one of his civil servants

00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:26.318
connects with
an old German friend,

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:28.779
carrying an all
important document

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.281
that could change
the course of history.

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:33.992
Don't look at it,
just put it under your arm,

00:00:34.076 --> 00:00:35.035
-God damnit.
-Right.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:37.746
-Make a terrible spy, Hugh.
-Sorry.

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:40.165
While the film
and the book are fiction,

00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:42.709
they are rooted
in real events in history.

00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:45.254
So, let's take a look
at the true story

00:00:45.337 --> 00:00:47.589
behind
Munich: The Edge of War.

00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:49.174
Adolf Hitler rose the power

00:00:49.258 --> 00:00:51.385
in the latter half
of the 1930s

00:00:51.468 --> 00:00:53.846
and was determined to invade
neighbouring countries

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:56.431
to reclaim
lost German territories.

00:00:56.515 --> 00:00:58.559
Having already marched
into Austria,

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:00.978
Hitler, set his eyes
on Czechoslovakia,

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:03.272
specifically,
the Sudetenland,

00:01:03.355 --> 00:01:04.356
which bordered Germany,

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:05.858
and where
many Germans lived.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:07.609
The Czech government
at the time

00:01:07.693 --> 00:01:09.027
was against annexing

00:01:09.111 --> 00:01:10.362
these borderlands
to Germany.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.990
Meanwhile, the British
and French were intent

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:16.118
on avoiding another war
and adopted a policy

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:17.953
of appeasing
Hitler's Germany.

00:01:18.537 --> 00:01:20.247
It was then Prime Minister
Neville Chamberlain,

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.000
portrayed by Jeremy Irons
in the film,

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.586
who took the lead
on the growing crisis.

00:01:25.669 --> 00:01:27.921
He met with Hitler
a total of three times.

00:01:28.005 --> 00:01:31.216
The first,
on 13th September, 1938,

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.218
at Hitler's residence
in Berchtesgaden.

00:01:33.302 --> 00:01:35.888
The second took place
in Bad Godesberg

00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:37.139
on the 24th,

00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:39.349
resulting in
the Godesberg memorandum,

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:41.018
which demanded
that Czechoslovakia

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:42.895
give up
the Sudetenland to Germany

00:01:42.978 --> 00:01:44.521
by 28th September.

00:01:44.605 --> 00:01:47.232
If they did not agree,
Hitler threatened

00:01:47.316 --> 00:01:49.067
to take the territories
by force.

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:52.029
Mere hours
before the deadline passed,

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.323
Chamberlain managed
to convince Italy's leader,

00:01:54.406 --> 00:01:56.783
Benito Mussolini,
to enter the negotiations

00:01:56.867 --> 00:01:59.786
and urge Hitler
to delay any hostilities.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.498
If Herr Hitler is not
going to listen to me,

00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:08.462
maybe he'll listen
to his great friend,

00:02:09.296 --> 00:02:11.215
Benito Mussolini.

00:02:12.508 --> 00:02:13.550
Hitler agreed to a delay

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:15.010
and an emergency meeting

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:17.179
between Britain, France,
Germany, and Italy

00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:20.307
was set for 29th September
in Munich.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.143
Even though this meeting
was about Czechoslovakia,

00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:24.853
their government
had no input.

00:02:24.937 --> 00:02:26.522
As we see in the film,

00:02:26.605 --> 00:02:28.065
Chamberlain received news
of this conference

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.191
from his personal secretary

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:31.151
while he was
addressing parliament.

00:02:31.735 --> 00:02:35.697
I am able to report
to the House,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.536
that I have just
received news from Berlin

00:02:40.619 --> 00:02:45.582
that Herr Hitler
has postponed mobilisation.

00:02:45.666 --> 00:02:47.793
Given the urgency
of the situation,

00:02:47.876 --> 00:02:49.211
it's not surprising
this meeting

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.547
did not take long
to reach a resolution.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:52.548
As pictured here,

00:02:52.631 --> 00:02:54.383
those in attendance
included Chamberlain,

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:55.676
Hitler, Mussolini,

00:02:55.759 --> 00:02:58.178
and French Prime Minister
Édouard Daladier.

00:02:58.929 --> 00:03:00.889
In the small hours
of 30th September,

00:03:00.973 --> 00:03:03.016
the Munich Agreement
was signed,

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.184
agreeing
to German occupation

00:03:04.268 --> 00:03:05.727
of the Sudetenland,

00:03:05.811 --> 00:03:08.438
as well as de facto control
of the rest of the country,

00:03:08.522 --> 00:03:11.358
on the agreement
Hitler would go no further.

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:12.484
Which, we know now,

00:03:12.568 --> 00:03:14.236
was not
what he intended to do.

00:03:15.112 --> 00:03:17.823
Czechoslovakia
had no option but to accept,

00:03:18.407 --> 00:03:20.576
knowing they could not fight
the Nazi army

00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:22.828
without the support
of Britain or France.

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:25.205
Shortly after the Munich
Agreement was signed,

00:03:25.289 --> 00:03:26.540
Chamberlain
approached Hitler

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.750
to sign
a non-aggression statement,

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:31.128
symbolic of the desire
of the two countries

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:33.422
to never go to war
with one another again.

00:03:33.505 --> 00:03:34.548
I want him

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.633
to publicly commit himself
to peace.

00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:38.844
The news
of a diplomatic resolution

00:03:38.927 --> 00:03:40.554
was met with relief.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:42.973
with Europe still scarred
by the First World War,

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:44.766
then known as the Great War,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.685
the prospect
of another conflict

00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:48.562
weighed heavily
on the leaders of the time.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:49.897
I fear

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.065
for the spiritual wellbeing
of our people,

00:03:52.149 --> 00:03:53.901
if they don't see
their leaders

00:03:53.984 --> 00:03:56.069
doing everything,
absolutely everything,

00:03:56.153 --> 00:03:58.864
to prevent another conflict.

00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:00.574
As Chamberlain
returned to London,

00:04:00.657 --> 00:04:02.326
waving
the non-aggression pact

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:03.911
signed by himself
and Hitler,

00:04:03.994 --> 00:04:06.455
it seemed
like war had been averted.

00:04:06.538 --> 00:04:09.625
He was met in London
with scenes of jubilance,

00:04:09.708 --> 00:04:10.876
cheered by thousands

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:12.044
as he arrived
at Buckingham Palace

00:04:12.127 --> 00:04:14.880
to give a first-hand report
to King George VI.

00:04:14.963 --> 00:04:17.591
Chamberlain later addressed
a similarly cheering crowd

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:19.676
from a second floor window
at Number 10,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.596
declaring, "I believe
it is peace for our time".

00:04:23.138 --> 00:04:24.848
But that wasn't the case.

00:04:24.932 --> 00:04:26.517
By March, 1939,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.852
Hitler had annexed
the rest of Czechoslovakia

00:04:28.936 --> 00:04:29.770
and in September

00:04:29.853 --> 00:04:32.564
the Nazi army
marched into Poland.

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:34.691
The next time Chamberlain
addressed the country,

00:04:35.192 --> 00:04:37.110
it was to call
for a declaration of war

00:04:37.194 --> 00:04:38.320
against Germany.

00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:39.446
Shortly afterwards,

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:40.864
he was forced to resign

00:04:40.948 --> 00:04:42.908
and was replaced
by Winston Churchill.

00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:45.077
While the Munich Declaration
went on to be seen

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.620
by many
of Churchill supporters

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:49.289
as an unnecessary
appeasement of Hitler,

00:04:49.373 --> 00:04:50.582
some historians have argued

00:04:50.666 --> 00:04:52.751
Chamberlain bought
much needed time

00:04:52.835 --> 00:04:55.003
to bolster
British military defences

00:04:55.087 --> 00:04:56.255
by delaying the war.

00:04:56.338 --> 00:04:58.924
Either way, the war
Chamberlain sought to avoid

00:04:59.007 --> 00:05:00.676
did eventually come to pass.

00:05:01.218 --> 00:05:02.302
To find out how

00:05:02.386 --> 00:05:03.595
this pivotal moment
in history pans out

00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:06.348
for the characters
of Munich: The Edge of War,

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:07.474
make sure
you catch the movie

00:05:07.558 --> 00:05:09.601
now streaming on Netflix.

